===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂ews-|id=dawn}}
From {{inh|en|enm|aire}}, from {{der|en|fro|air}}, from {{der|en|la|āēr}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἀήρ}}. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|lyft}}. More at [[lift]], [[loft]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[substance]] constituting [[Earth]]'s [[atmosphere]]: a [[gaseous]] mixture of [[nitrogen]], [[oxygen]], and various trace gases.
#: {{ux|en|This drill runs on compressed '''air'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|As you ascend, the '''air''' gets thinner.}}
## The substance of the atmosphere seen as an agency of freshness.
##: {{ux|en|I'm going outside to get some '''air'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|Open the window and let some '''air''' into the room.}}
## {{lb|en|historical|philosophy|alchemy}} One of the four [[element]]s of the [[ancient]] [[Greek]]s and [[Roman]]s.
##: {{syn|en|wind}}
## {{lb|en|historical|medical}} A [[local]] environment or atmosphere, in the context of its effects on [[behavior]], [[health]], [[weather]], etc.
##* {{quote-text|en|date=May 12 1991|chapter=[[w:List of Jeeves and Wooster episodes|Kidnapped!]]|title=w:Jeeves and Wooster|section=Series 2, Episode 5
|passage=''Jeeves:'' Foreign travel often liberates emotions best kept in check, sir. The '''air''' of North America is notoriously stimulating in this regard, as witness the [[w:United States Declaration of Independence|regrettable behavior]] of its inhabitants in 1776.<br>''B. Wooster:'' Hm? What happened in 1776, Jeeves?<br>''Jeeves:'' I prefer not to dwell on it, if it's convenient to you, sir.}}
##: {{ux|en|There was a tension in the '''air''' which made me suspect an approaching storm.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|loosely}} The substance of the atmosphere on a planet other than Earth.
#: {{ux|en|The '''air''' on Mars is very thin and consists mostly of carbon dioxide.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|usually|with '''the'''}} The apparently open [[space]] above the [[ground]] which this substance fills, {{lb|en|historical}} formerly thought to be limited by the [[firmament]] but {{lb|en|meteorology}} now considered to be surrounded by the near-[[vacuum]] of [[outer space]].
#: {{ux|en|The flock of birds took to the '''air'''.}}
# A [[breeze]]; a gentle [[wind]].
# A [[feeling]] or [[sense]].
#: {{co|en|to give it an '''air''' of artistry and [[sophistication]]}}
#* {{RQ:Chesnutt House Behind the Cedars|I|passage=The girl stooped to pluck a rose, and as she bent over it, her profile was clearly outlined. She held the flower to her face with a long-drawn inhalation, then went up the steps, crossed the piazza, opened the door without knocking, and entered the house with the '''air''' of one thoroughly at home.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=October|author=H. A. Vallance|title=Across Denmark by Lyntog|journal=Railway Magazine|page=658|text=Even at the busiest periods, an '''air''' of quiet orderliness pervades the hall, and the first impression gained by the traveller is one of efficiency, neatness and cleanliness.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1968
|author=w:Robert Conquest
|chapter=The Purge Begins
|title={{w|The Great Terror (book)|The Great Terror: Stalin's Purge of the Thirties}}
|url=https://archive.org/details/greatterrorstali0000conq/
|publisher=w:Macmillan Company
|LCCN=68-17513
|OCLC=1169910711
|OL=21272570M
|page=77
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/greatterrorstali0000conq/page/77/
|text=But the dull, cool, calculating effect given cumulatively through Stalin’s long career, the '''air''' of a great glacier moving slowly and by the easiest path to overwhelm some Alpine valley, is only part of the picture. At various times - and especially in his early career - the calm of his general manner was broken, and expression given to the driving emotions that possessed him.}}
#* '''November 2 2014''', Daniel Taylor, "[http://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/nov/02/manchester-city-manchester-united-premier-league-match-report Sergio Agüero strike wins derby for Manchester City against 10-man United]," ''guardian.co.uk''
#*: Smalling’s quick one-two of yellow cards towards the end of the first half had left an '''air''' of inevitability about what would follow and, if anything, it was probably a surprise that City restricted themselves to Sergio Agüero’s goal bearing in mind another of United’s defenders, Marcos Rojo, was taken off on a stretcher early in the second half with a dislocated shoulder.
## A sense of [[poise]], [[graciousness]], or [[quality]].
##* {{RQ:Austen Emma|I|4|text="He is very plain, undoubtedly&mdash;remarkably plain:&mdash;but that is nothing compared with his entire want of gentility. I had no right to expect much, and I did not expect much; but I had no idea that he could be so very clownish, so totally without '''air'''. I had imagined him, I confess, a degree or two nearer gentility."}}
## {{lb|en|usually|in the plural}} [[pretentious|Pretension]]; [[snobby|snobbishness]]; [[pretence]] that one is better than others.
##: {{co|en|putting on '''airs'''}}
##* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=1|passage=He read the letter aloud. Sophia listened with the studied '''air''' of one for whom, even in these days, a title possessed some surreptitious allurement.}}
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[melody]] or [[song]], especially a solo; an [[aria]].
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|18|text="If I," said Mr. Collins, "were so fortunate as to be able to sing, I should have great pleasure, I am sure, in obliging the company with an '''air'''; for I consider music as a very innocent diversion, and perfectly compatible with the profession of a clergyman{{...}}"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Sketches of Life and Character|passage=The lad, his son, had obtained a Jew's-harp, and learned to play upon it the profane '''airs''' of "Yankee Doodle," "Hail Columbia," "St. Patrick's Day," and "Auld Lang Syne."|year=1850|author=T. S. Arthur|page=70|authorlink=Timothy Shay Arthur|pageurl=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Sketches_of_Life_and_Character/mB0tAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA70|chapter=Deacon Smith and his Violin|oclc=1087079145|publisher=J. W. Bradley|location=Philadelphia|url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Sketches_of_Life_and_Character/mB0tAAAAYAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} [[nothing|Nothing]]; absence of anything.
# {{lb|en|countable|uncountable}} An [[air conditioner|air conditioning system]].
#: {{ux|en|Could you turn on the '''air'''?}}
#: {{ux|en|Hey, did you mean to leave the '''airs''' on all week while you were on vacation?}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|: |chemistry}} Any specific [[gas]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable|snowboarding|skateboarding|motor sports}} The state of being briefly [[airborne]] during a jump.
# A television or radio signal; {{lb|en|by extension}} [[media]] [[broadcast]]s in general.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|title=Selling the Air |author=Thomas Streeter |page= |ISBN=0226777227 |passage=Ernst gave a list of political activists who had been denied access to the air by private broadcasters, and pointed out that "Secretary Hoover's signature in New York sells for $150,000 to $200,000," thus limiting access to the '''air''' on the part of labor unions and other underrepresented groups. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|title=The Singing of the Dead |author=Dana Stabenow |page=17 |ISBN= 0312209576|passage=Coming to you live once a month, or whenever I feel like broadcasting a little pirate '''air'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=Gary Andres; Paul Hernnson|title=Lobbying Reconsidered: Politics Under the Influence|page=149
|passage=“These members need '''air''' cover in the media.” Paid media is the admission ticket to enter the big-time Washington stage.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[publicity|Publicity]].

