====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bring (something) into contact with the air, so as to [[freshen]] or [[dry]] it.
#: {{ux|en|I'll hang these clothes on the rack to '''air''' them.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To let fresh air into (a room or a building), to [[ventilate]].
#: {{ux|en|It's getting quite [[stuffy]] in this room: let's open the windows and '''air''' it.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[make]] [[public]] (an [[opinion]], concern, issue, secret, differences, etc); to present to public view (and sometimes discussion).
#: {{ux|en|Residents used the public meeting to '''air''' their concerns about the poor state of the roads.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2010-02-11|author=Rosie Swash|title=John Mayer apologises after using N-word in Playboy interview|journal=w:The Guardian|url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2010/feb/11/john-mayer-playboy-interview|passage=John Mayer has apologised for using a racist epithet in a recent interview with Playboy magazine, after a number of prominent black musicians '''aired''' their disapproval.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1917|journal=w:National Geographic|section=[[s:National Geographic Magazine/Volume 31/Number 3/Russia's Democrats|v.31, March 1917]]
|passage=Thus, in spite of all opposition, the rural and urban assemblies retained the germ of local government, and in spite of the dual control, as the result of which much of their influence was nullified, they did have a certain value in '''airing''' abuses and suggesting improvements.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[broadcast]] (a television show etc.).
#: {{ux|en|The BBC decided not to '''air''' the controversial episode.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[broadcast]].
#: {{ux|en|This game show first '''aired''' in the 1990s and is still going today.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|British|MLE|slang}} To [[ignore]] (a person).
#: {{ux|en|Why is this girl '''airing''' me?}}

